# Footer

A footer offers consistent supporting links at the end of a standalone site.

These are civica reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use it for the design-system website or a separate service page. Include useful accessibility, help and information-handling links without overloading the end of a task.

## When not to use it

Do not inject a website footer into every Home Assistant card or mobile-app view. NHS guidance itself distinguishes web pages from mobile apps. A footer is not a place for critical device controls.

## Home Assistant adaptation

Use identity, licence and ownership information appropriate to the page. Project documentation uses civica; a separate household or business page may use its own chosen name and authorised artwork. Do not copy NHS or Crown copyright claims. Help links must lead to a known destination and appear consistently. An application’s native settings and about screens may be the appropriate home for equivalent information.

## Documentation site imprint

The documentation footer permanently states that civica is independent of the UK Government and uses open-source GOV.UK Frontend components. Its **Licence and attribution** link opens the [Licence page](../community/licensing.html), which renders the repository's `LICENSE.md` during each build. The statement remains available after the first-visit notice is hidden.

Keep this as one concise sentence. Allow it to wrap on narrow screens rather than reducing its font size or forcing a single visual line. Link the licence name directly to the complete terms and attribution.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use a footer landmark and label any navigation inside it. Keep visible focus, readable link text and enough space between targets. A large footer can use grouped headings, but preserve a sensible reading order when columns stack.

